      "dom_id": "question-132399",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Which digits, when inserted into the blank, make the number $35\\,832\\,4◻2$ divisible by $6?$\n\nI. $6$\n\nII. $7$\n\nIII. $9$",
      "worked_solution": "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$ Remember that to determine whether a number is divisible by $3,$ we check whether the sum of the digits is divisible by $3.$\n\nFor the given number, the sum of the digits is\n\n$$\n3+5+8+3+2+4+◻+2=27+◻.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each of the given digits we can substitute into the blank.\n\n- If we substitute $6$ into the blank, we get $35\\,832\\,462,$ which is divisible by $2.$ Then the sum of the digits is $27+6=33,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\n- If we substitute $7$ into the blank, we get $35\\,832\\,472,$ which is divisible by $2.$ However, it is not divisible by $3$ because the sum of the digits is $27+7=34,$ which is not divisible by $3.$ So, the number is *not* divisible by $6$ either.\n\n- If we substitute $9$ into the blank, we get $35\\,832\\,492,$ which is divisible by $2.$ Then the sum of the digits is $27+9=36,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"",
