      "name": null,
      "problem": "Which digits, when inserted into the blank, make the number $11\\,521\\,6◻6$ divisible by $6?$\n\nI. $3$\n\nII. $7$\n\nIII. $9$",
      "worked_solution": "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$ Remember that to determine whether a number is divisible by $3,$ we check whether the sum of the digits is divisible by $3.$\n\nFor the given number, the sum of the digits is\n\n$$\n1+1+5+2+1+6+◻+6=22+◻.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each of the given digits we can substitute into the blank.\n\n- If we substitute $3$ into the blank, we get $11\\,521\\,636,$ which is divisible by $2.$ However, it is not divisible by $3$ because the sum of the digits is $22+3=25,$ which is not divisible by $3.$ So, the number is *not* divisible by $6$ either.\n\n- If we substitute $7$ into the blank, we get $11\\,521\\,676,$ which is divisible by $2.$ However, it is not divisible by $3$ because the sum of the digits is $22+7=29,$ which is not divisible by $3.$ So, the number is *not* divisible by $6$ either.\n\n- If we substitute $9$ into the blank, we get $11\\,521\\,696,$ which is divisible by $2.$ However, it is not divisible by $3$ because the sum of the digits is $22+9=31,$ which is not divisible by $3.$ So, the number is *not* divisible by $6$ either.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"None.\"",
