      "problem": "Which digits, when inserted into the blank, make the number $66\\,333\\,9◻0$ divisible by $6?$\n\nI. $0$\n\nII. $3$\n\nIII. $9$",
      "worked_solution": "A number is divisible by $6$ if it is divisible by both $2$ and $3.$ Remember that to determine whether a number is divisible by $3,$ we check whether the sum of the digits is divisible by $3.$\n\nFor the given number, the sum of the digits is\n\n$$\n6+6+3+3+3+9+◻+0=30+◻.\n$$\n\nNow, let's consider each of the given digits we can substitute into the blank.\n\n- If we substitute $0$ into the blank, we get $66\\,333\\,900,$ which is divisible by $2.$ Then the sum of the digits is $30+0=30,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\n- If we substitute $3$ into the blank, we get $66\\,333\\,930,$ which is divisible by $2.$ Then the sum of the digits is $30+3=33,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\n- If we substitute $9$ into the blank, we get $66\\,333\\,990,$ which is divisible by $2.$ Then the sum of the digits is $30+9=39,$ which is divisible by $3,$ so the number is also divisible by $3.$ Therefore, the number is divisible by $6.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I, II, and III.\"",
